### Why is returning 200 with success false an anti-pattern?

Returning 200 with `{"success": false}` breaks three systems at once. HTTP libraries treat 2xx as success and skip error paths, so the application silently mishandles failures. Monitoring dashboards count HTTP status, so a wall of fake 200s hides a real outage and SLO alerts never fire. Retry logic decides to retry based on status, so a 200 is never retried even if the real failure was a transient 503.

```java
// Anti-pattern: 200 for everything, error hidden in body.
@PostMapping("/transfers")
public Map<String, Object> transfer(@RequestBody TransferRequest req) {
    try {
        transferService.create(req);
        return Map.of("success", true);
    } catch (Exception e) {
        return Map.of("success", false, "error", e.getMessage()); // 200 + error!
    }
}
// Problem: monitoring stays green, clients don't trigger error paths,
// retry logic never retries, caches may cache the "error" response.
```

### What makes a correct status code?

The status code must reflect the failure category. The server should never collapse different failures into one code.

| Status | Meaning | Example |
|---|---|---|
| 400 | Bad Request: malformed or missing fields | Invalid JSON, missing required field |
| 401 | Unauthorized: not authenticated | Missing or expired token |
| 403 | Forbidden: authenticated but not permitted | Customer accessing admin report |
| 404 | Not Found: resource does not exist | Account SAV9999 |
| 409 | Conflict: state conflict | Duplicate account creation |
| 422 | Unprocessable Entity: business-rule failure | Insufficient funds on valid request |
| 429 | Too Many Requests: rate limited | Client exceeded call quota |
| 500 | Internal Server Error: unexpected crash | Null pointer, database down |

```java
// Correct: each failure maps to the right status code.
// InsufficientFundsException  → 422 Unprocessable Entity
// AccountNotFoundException     → 404 Not Found
// DuplicateAccountException    → 409 Conflict
// MethodArgumentNotValid       → 400 Bad Request
// AuthenticationException      → 401 Unauthorized
// AccessDeniedException        → 403 Forbidden
```

### What makes a good error body?

A good error body is consistent across every endpoint so clients write one parser. It separates a stable machine-readable code from a human-readable message, carries a trace id, a timestamp, and never includes stack traces.

```java
package com.bank.exception;

import java.time.Instant;

public record ApiError(
        String code,        // STABLE: "INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS" – clients switch on this
        String message,     // Human-readable, can change or be localised
        String traceId,     // Correlation id to find logs
        Instant timestamp   // When the error occurred
) {}
```

### Why separate the error code from the message?

The stable code like `INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS` never changes, so client logic can switch on it reliably. The human message like "your balance is too low" can be reworded or localised freely. Changing the message must never break a client. The team learned this the hard way when the mobile app switched on the message text and a marketing rewording broke the app overnight.

```java
// Client code switches on the stable code, not the message.
if ("INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS".equals(error.code())) {
    showAddMoneyButton();
}
// The message can change from "Insufficient funds" to "Your balance is too low"
// without breaking any client.
```

### Why include a trace id and exclude stack traces?

The trace id appears in both the error response and the server logs, so support can take the id from the customer and find the exact request. Stack traces must never appear in the response because they leak class names, framework versions, and internal structure that help attackers and confuse clients. Stack traces go to the logs under the trace id.

```java
// Trace id in the response: customer can share it with support.
// {
//   "code": "INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS",
//   "message": "Balance 250000 is less than requested 900000",
//   "traceId": "a1b2c3d4-e5f6-7890",
//   "timestamp": "2026-03-15T10:30:00Z"
// }

// Stack trace in the logs (NOT in the response):
// [traceId=a1b2c3d4] InsufficientFundsException: Balance 250000 < 900000
//   at com.bank.service.TransferService.validate(TransferService.java:45)
```

### How do you centralise error handling with @RestControllerAdvice?

Instead of try-catch in every controller, one advice class maps each exception type to a status and a body. This guarantees every endpoint returns the same shape and the same rules.

```java
package com.bank.exception;

import org.springframework.http.*;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import org.slf4j.MDC;
import java.time.Instant;

@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(InsufficientFundsException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiError> handleInsufficient(InsufficientFundsException e) {
        return buildError("INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS", e.getMessage(),
                HttpStatus.UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY);
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(AccountNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiError> handleNotFound(AccountNotFoundException e) {
        return buildError("ACCOUNT_NOT_FOUND", e.getMessage(),
                HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND);
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(DuplicateAccountException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiError> handleConflict(DuplicateAccountException e) {
        return buildError("DUPLICATE_ACCOUNT", e.getMessage(),
                HttpStatus.CONFLICT);
    }

    private ResponseEntity<ApiError> buildError(String code, String message,
                                                 HttpStatus status) {
        ApiError error = new ApiError(code, message,
                MDC.get("traceId"), Instant.now());
        return ResponseEntity.status(status).body(error);
    }
}
```

### How does this compare to RFC 9457 Problem Details?

RFC 9457 Problem Details is the IETF standard for error bodies, using `Content-Type: application/problem+json`. It has the same idea: a stable `type` URL, a `title`, a `status`, a `detail`, and an `instance`. Spring supports it natively with the `ProblemDetail` class. You can use Problem Details instead of a custom `ApiError` record for a standards-based approach.

```java
// RFC 9457 Problem Details via Spring's ProblemDetail.
@ExceptionHandler(InsufficientFundsException.class)
public ProblemDetail handle(InsufficientFundsException e) {
    ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(
            HttpStatus.UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY, e.getMessage());
    pd.setType(java.net.URI.create("https://api.nbi.in/problems/insufficient-funds"));
    pd.setTitle("Insufficient Funds");
    pd.setProperty("traceId", MDC.get("traceId"));
    return pd; // Content-Type: application/problem+json automatically
}
```

### What are common mistakes in error handling?

Returning 200 for errors. Leaking stack traces in production. Putting try-catch in every controller instead of centralising. Using the human message as a machine code. Not including a trace id.

```java
// Wrong: try-catch in each controller, stack trace leaked, no trace id.
@PostMapping("/transfers")
public ResponseEntity<?> create(@RequestBody TransferRequest req) {
    try {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(transferService.create(req));
    } catch (Exception e) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(500)
                .body(Map.of("error", e.toString())); // Stack trace leaked!
    }
}

// Correct: let exceptions bubble up to centralised @RestControllerAdvice.
@PostMapping("/transfers")
public ResponseEntity<TransferResponse> create(
        @Valid @RequestBody TransferRequest req) {
    TransferResponse resp = transferService.create(req);
    return ResponseEntity.created(java.net.URI.create("/api/v1/transfers/" + resp.id()))
            .body(resp);
    // Exceptions are caught by GlobalExceptionHandler automatically.
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know about error design?

The stable error code is part of the API contract, just like URLs. Once clients switch on `INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS`, you can never rename it without breaking them, so choose codes carefully and document them. The human message is free to change. This separation lets you improve user-facing wording for years without a single client-breaking change.
